#
# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the test suite.
#
# EVERY ENTRY HERE IS A LIBRARY THAT ALLOCATES ONCE AND KEEPS IT for the life of
# the process: GTK's type system and settings, GLib's quarks and thread pools,
# GnuTLS's globals, p11-kit's module registry. None of them is a leak this
# repository can fix and all of them would otherwise bury the ones it can.
#
# NOTHING IN src/ IS SUPPRESSED, on purpose. A leak reported against this
# backend's own code is a defect, and the cancellation tests exist precisely to
# provoke that class of defect.
#
# AND NOTHING BROAD ENOUGH TO COVER src/ EITHER, which is the trap this file fell
# into. LeakSanitizer matches a suppression against ANY frame of the allocation
# stack, not the innermost one -- and every allocation in this codebase passes
# through g_malloc, g_strdup or g_object_new. So `leak:libglib-2.0`,
# `leak:libgio-2.0` and `leak:libgobject-2.0` did not suppress "GLib's one-time
# allocations", they suppressed almost every leak this backend can have,
# including the ones the cancellation suite was written to catch. They are gone;
# a real leak of a g_strdup() in src/ is reported again.
#
# What is left is either a library with its own allocator and its own frames
# (GTK, GnuTLS, p11-kit, Pango, fontconfig, the GL stacks, dconf), or a NAMED
# one-shot function that cannot appear in any path of ours.
leak:libgtk-4
leak:libgdk
leak:libgnutls
leak:libp11-kit
leak:libpango
leak:libfontconfig
leak:libEGL
leak:libGL
leak:dconf
leak:g_type_register_static
leak:g_type_add_interface_static
leak:g_quark_init
leak:g_quark_from_static_string
# GLib's own singletons, by name rather than by module: the thread pools that
# run every GTask worker, the default GMainContext, and the GIO module registry
# are all allocated once and freed never, by design.
leak:g_thread_pool_new
leak:g_private_set_alloc0
leak:g_io_module_new
leak:g_dbus_error_register_error_domain
# GDBus's WIRE DECODER, on GDBus's own worker thread. A message that has been
# parsed but not yet dispatched when the process exits is reported as leaked;
# it belongs to a connection this suite deliberately does not drain. Named
# rather than `leak:libgio-2.0`, because that module name also covers every
# g_task_*, g_dbus_* and GVariant allocation this backend makes.
leak:g_dbus_message_new_from_blob
